<!--
  Mini-SOC custom rules  (installed to /var/ossec/etc/rules/minisoc_rules.xml)

  Custom rule IDs must be between 100000 and 120000.
  Alerts with level >= 10 are forwarded to DFIR-IRIS (see ossec.conf).
-->
<group name="minisoc,">

  <!-- Suricata: the stock rule 86601 gives every IDS alert level 3, which is too
       low to stand out. Re-rate them by Suricata's own severity
       (1 = high, 2 = medium, 3 = low).

       Excludes soc (10.10.10.20): its Greenbone scanner's own vulnerability
       checks (exploit-pattern probes, default-credential brute force, etc.)
       otherwise look exactly like a real attacker to the IDS and drown out
       everything else - see rules 100122/100123 below for the equivalent
       exclusion on the web-log side, and the "Problems solved" writeup for
       why this isn't just filtered out entirely (you still want to know
       your own scanner is doing its job; you just don't want it paged as
       an incident).

       Found 2026-10-20, while building the SQLi exercise, that 100100,
       100101 and 100110 (below) had never actually fired once in this
       project's history despite being claimed "verified end to end" -
       the built-in <srcip> tag only reads Wazuh's own decoded srcip
       attribute, which only exists if a decoder explicitly maps into it
       via <order>; the generic "json" decoder Wazuh uses for Suricata's
       eve.json just creates a plain dynamic field named src_ip (with the
       underscore, straight from the JSON key) instead, which <srcip>
       never sees. An unmatched <srcip negate="yes"> doesn't fail open
       (match anyway) - it fails closed, so every single event silently
       never matched. Fixed by matching the real field directly with
       <field name="src_ip" negate="yes">. Confirmed with wazuh-logtest:
       both the escalation (severity 1/2 from a non-excluded source) and
       the exclusion (same severities from 10.10.10.20) now behave
       correctly, and a real SQLi request now lands as 100100 (level 12)
       instead of silently staying at the generic level-3 base rule. -->
  <rule id="100100" level="12">
    <if_sid>86601</if_sid>
    <field name="alert.severity">^1$</field>
    <field name="src_ip" negate="yes">^10\.10\.10\.20$</field>
    <description>Suricata [high]: $(alert.signature)</description>
    <group>ids,minisoc_ids,</group>
  </rule>

  <rule id="100101" level="10">
    <if_sid>86601</if_sid>
    <field name="alert.severity">^2$</field>
    <field name="src_ip" negate="yes">^10\.10\.10\.20$</field>
    <description>Suricata [medium]: $(alert.signature)</description>
    <group>ids,minisoc_ids,</group>
  </rule>

  <rule id="100102" level="5">
    <if_sid>86601</if_sid>
    <field name="alert.severity">^3$</field>
    <description>Suricata [low]: $(alert.signature)</description>
    <group>ids,minisoc_ids,</group>
  </rule>

  <!-- Port scan: many IDS hits from one source in a short time. Excludes
       soc (10.10.10.20) for the same reason as above - a Greenbone scan
       generates far more than 15 IDS hits/minute by design. -->
  <rule id="100110" level="10" frequency="15" timeframe="60">
    <if_matched_group>minisoc_ids</if_matched_group>
    <same_field>src_ip</same_field>
    <field name="src_ip" negate="yes">^10\.10\.10\.20$</field>
    <description>Possible network scan: many IDS alerts from $(src_ip)</description>
    <mitre><id>T1046</id></mitre>
    <group>ids,recon,minisoc_ids_correlated,</group>
  </rule>

  <!-- Same exclusion on the web-log side: Wazuh's stock rules 31151-31163
       (0245-web_rules.xml) are the complete family of "multiple X from the
       same source ip in a short window" correlations - they all fire
       heavily on Greenbone's own vulnerability-scan traffic against target
       (it tests SQLi, XSS, and deliberately triggers 4xx/5xx errors, among
       other things), not just the two caught in the first pass
       (100122/100123 below). In testing, 83% of all alerts (4130 of 4984)
       were soc scanning itself, not an attacker. Downgraded rather than
       silenced: still visible in Wazuh at a low level with a label that
       says what it actually is, just not escalated to DFIR-IRIS as an
       incident. -->
  <rule id="100122" level="3">
    <if_sid>31151</if_sid>
    <srcip>10.10.10.20</srcip>
    <description>Greenbone vulnerability scan traffic (expected, not an incident): web server 400 errors</description>
    <group>minisoc_scanner_noise,</group>
  </rule>

  <rule id="100123" level="3">
    <if_sid>31153</if_sid>
    <srcip>10.10.10.20</srcip>
    <description>Greenbone vulnerability scan traffic (expected, not an incident): common web attack patterns</description>
    <group>minisoc_scanner_noise,</group>
  </rule>

  <rule id="100124" level="3">
    <if_sid>31152</if_sid>
    <srcip>10.10.10.20</srcip>
    <description>Greenbone vulnerability scan traffic (expected, not an incident): SQL injection probes</description>
    <group>minisoc_scanner_noise,</group>
  </rule>

  <rule id="100125" level="3">
    <if_sid>31154</if_sid>
    <srcip>10.10.10.20</srcip>
    <description>Greenbone vulnerability scan traffic (expected, not an incident): XSS probes</description>
    <group>minisoc_scanner_noise,</group>
  </rule>

  <rule id="100126" level="3">
    <if_sid>31161</if_sid>
    <srcip>10.10.10.20</srcip>
    <description>Greenbone vulnerability scan traffic (expected, not an incident): web server 501 errors</description>
    <group>minisoc_scanner_noise,</group>
  </rule>

  <rule id="100127" level="3">
    <if_sid>31162</if_sid>
    <srcip>10.10.10.20</srcip>
    <description>Greenbone vulnerability scan traffic (expected, not an incident): web server 500 errors</description>
    <group>minisoc_scanner_noise,</group>
  </rule>

  <rule id="100128" level="3">
    <if_sid>31163</if_sid>
    <srcip>10.10.10.20</srcip>
    <description>Greenbone vulnerability scan traffic (expected, not an incident): web server 503 errors</description>
    <group>minisoc_scanner_noise,</group>
  </rule>

  <!-- Same Greenbone-noise problem, found in two more stock rules outside the
       0245-web_rules.xml same_source_ip family the first two passes covered:
       its web NVTs include a literal Shellshock payload test (31168/31169,
       CVE-2014-6271/6278, level 15 - the single highest severity in the
       stock ruleset) and its SSH NVT probes a long list of common
       usernames/products that don't exist on this box (ospd-openvas, karaf,
       etc.), which is exactly what 5712 ("brute force... non existent user")
       is built to catch. Found 2026-10-21 from a live Wazuh alert count after
       actually running the exercises - 10 of that day's level>=10 alerts
       were these two, all sourced from soc (10.10.10.20), none of them an
       actual incident. Same fix as 100122-100128: downgrade, don't silence. -->
  <rule id="100131" level="3">
    <if_sid>31168</if_sid>
    <srcip>10.10.10.20</srcip>
    <description>Greenbone vulnerability scan traffic (expected, not an incident): Shellshock probe</description>
    <group>minisoc_scanner_noise,</group>
  </rule>

  <rule id="100132" level="3">
    <if_sid>31169</if_sid>
    <srcip>10.10.10.20</srcip>
    <description>Greenbone vulnerability scan traffic (expected, not an incident): Shellshock probe</description>
    <group>minisoc_scanner_noise,</group>
  </rule>

  <!-- 5712 is itself a frequency/correlation rule (if_matched_sid + same_source_ip
       + its own frequency/timeframe), not a plain per-line rule - chaining
       <if_matched_sid>5712</if_matched_sid> onto it the way <if_sid> chains onto
       a normal rule does NOT replace its alert the way the 31151-31163/31168/31169
       chains above do; it silently never fires at all (no frequency/timeframe of
       its own to evaluate against). Confirmed directly: a first attempt using
       exactly that shape never appeared in /var/ossec/logs/alerts/alerts.json even
       once, while the stock 5712 kept firing at full level 10 for Greenbone's own
       traffic regardless. Fixed the only way that actually works for a frequency
       rule - override 5712 itself (overwrite="yes", a supported, official Wazuh
       mechanism for customizing a specific stock rule without touching the
       ruleset file it ships in) to exclude Greenbone via a negated <srcip>, so it
       still fires normally for a real attacker (confirmed: exercises/03-ssh-bruteforce's
       own traffic, from kali not soc, is untouched by the exclusion), and give
       Greenbone's matching pattern its own independent frequency rule instead. -->
  <rule id="5712" level="10" frequency="8" timeframe="120" ignore="60" overwrite="yes">
    <if_matched_sid>5710</if_matched_sid>
    <same_source_ip />
    <srcip negate="yes">10.10.10.20</srcip>
    <description>sshd: brute force trying to get access to the system. Non existent user.</description>
    <mitre>
      <id>T1110</id>
    </mitre>
    <group>authentication_failures,gdpr_IV_35.7.d,gdpr_IV_32.2,hipaa_164.312.b,nist_800_53_SI.4,nist_800_53_AU.14,nist_800_53_AC.7,pci_dss_11.4,pci_dss_10.2.4,pci_dss_10.2.5,tsc_CC6.1,tsc_CC6.8,tsc_CC7.2,tsc_CC7.3,</group>
  </rule>

  <rule id="100133" level="3" frequency="8" timeframe="120" ignore="60">
    <if_matched_sid>5710</if_matched_sid>
    <same_source_ip />
    <srcip>10.10.10.20</srcip>
    <description>Greenbone vulnerability scan traffic (expected, not an incident): SSH default-credential probe against nonexistent usernames</description>
    <group>minisoc_scanner_noise,</group>
  </rule>

  <!-- vsftpd (see exercises/01-ftp-webshell): anonymous access is disabled by default,
       these only fire while that exercise's vulnerability is deliberately enabled.
       Chained onto Wazuh's built-in vsftpd rules (11402 OK LOGIN, 11404 OK UPLOAD,
       both from ruleset/rules/0185-vsftpd_rules.xml) rather than re-matching the
       log from scratch, since a standalone rule never gets evaluated once the
       built-in vsftpd decoder/rule chain has already claimed the event. -->
  <rule id="100120" level="10">
    <if_sid>11402</if_sid>
    <match>anon password</match>
    <description>FTP: anonymous login succeeded</description>
    <mitre><id>T1078.001</id></mitre>
    <group>ftp,minisoc_ftp,</group>
  </rule>

  <rule id="100121" level="12">
    <if_sid>11404</if_sid>
    <regex>\.php</regex>
    <description>FTP: a PHP file was uploaded via anonymous FTP - possible web shell!</description>
    <mitre><id>T1505.003</id></mitre>
    <group>ftp,web,minisoc_ftp_webshell,</group>
  </rule>

  <!-- DVWA login brute force (/legacy/login.php): Wazuh has no built-in
       decoder for a generic PHP login form the way it does for SSH/FTP, so
       without this, repeated failed logins against the hidden DVWA app are
       completely invisible - found by actually running a brute-force
       script from kali and checking: zero alerts fired. The access log
       doesn't carry the response body, and DVWA's login always replies
       302 whether the attempt succeeded or failed, so pass/fail per
       request can't be told apart from the log alone - the signal used
       here is volume instead: a real user POSTs to the login page once or
       twice, a brute force does it dozens of times in a row from the same
       source. Same if_matched_sid + same_source_ip frequency pattern as
       the stock 31151-31163 family above.

       Chained onto 31108 ("Ignored URLs (simple queries)"), not its parent
       31100 directly: a plain login.php POST has no suspicious query
       string, so the stock ruleset's own catch-all for boring traffic
       claims the event first, same as every attempt in testing. Wazuh
       only evaluates further down the branch that actually matched, not
       every other sibling chained on the same if_sid - a rule chained
       directly onto 31100 next to 31108 silently never fires, no error,
       just never shows up, found by testing with the rule's level
       deliberately raised above 31108's to rule out a level/priority
       explanation before suspecting the chain itself. -->
  <rule id="100129" level="3">
    <if_sid>31108</if_sid>
    <url>/legacy/login.php</url>
    <protocol>^POST$</protocol>
    <description>POST to the hidden portal's login page (/legacy/login.php)</description>
    <group>minisoc_web,</group>
  </rule>

  <rule id="100130" level="10" frequency="5" timeframe="60">
    <if_matched_sid>100129</if_matched_sid>
    <same_source_ip />
    <description>Possible brute force attack against the hidden portal's login page (/legacy/login.php)</description>
    <mitre><id>T1110</id></mitre>
    <group>authentication_failures,minisoc_web_brute_force,</group>
  </rule>

</group>
